          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Window Insulation Kits Work: The Still Air Gap Does the Insulating
          </h2>

          <p>
            A window insulation kit is a sheet of clear plastic film taped around the window frame to seal it off from the room. The film itself has almost no insulating value. What insulates is the layer of still air trapped between the film and the glass, which acts like a crude storm window, and the tape seal that stops drafts leaking around the sashes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Air Pocket and the Seal</h3>

          <p>
            Old single-pane and loose double-hung windows lose heat two ways: through the cold glass, and through air leaking around the sashes and frame. A taped film stops the second completely as long as the seal holds, and the trapped air slows the first. That is why a kit makes a drafty room feel noticeably warmer even though the plastic is thin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shrink Film vs Stretch Film</h3>

          <p>
            Most indoor kits use heat-shrink film. You press it onto double-sided tape, then warm it with a hair dryer until it tightens into a smooth, nearly invisible panel. Some outdoor kits, like the Frost King V96H, use stretch film that you pull taut as you fit it, with no hair dryer. Shrink film looks cleaner; stretch film is simpler outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Kit Fixes and What It Does Not</h3>

          <p>
            A kit fixes drafts through the sash, the cold-glass chill, and much of the condensation and frost that forms on the inside of cold glass, because warm, moist room air can no longer reach it. It does not fix rotted frames, gaps between the frame and the wall, or a failed exterior seal. Those need caulk; our guide to the <a href="/best-caulk-for-windows/">best caulk for windows</a> covers which to use. While you are sealing up for winter, the pipes in unheated crawlspaces and exterior walls deserve the same attention; our picks for the <a href="/best-pipe-insulation/">best pipe insulation</a> cover foam, rubber and fiberglass sleeves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who Benefits Most</h3>

          <p>
            Renters, owners of older homes with single-pane glass, and anyone with a few rooms that never warm up get the biggest return. On new, tight double- or triple-pane windows, the gain is small.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indoor-vs-outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Indoor vs Outdoor Window Insulation Kits: Indoor Is Right for Most Homes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kits are sold for indoor or outdoor installation, and the two are not interchangeable. For most homes, indoor is easier, safer, cleaner looking and protected from weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Indoor Kits</h3>

          <p>
            Indoor kits tape to the interior window trim or frame. You work from inside, at room temperature, with a hair dryer to shrink the film tight. The finished result is close to invisible, and the film is out of the wind and snow all winter. The 3M five-pack, both Duck kits and the Frost King V73/9H are indoor kits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoor Kits</h3>

          <p>
            Outdoor kits fasten around the outside of the window. They keep tape off interior paint and leave the inside free for blinds and curtains, but they need a dry day, a safe way to reach the window and a very careful seal, because wind works on the film all winter. The 3M Outdoor kit and Frost King V96H are outdoor kits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Outdoor Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            Choose outdoor when the indoor trim is delicate, freshly painted or too narrow to hold tape, when indoor frames are cluttered with built-in blinds, or on a ground-floor window that you can reach safely from outside. Upper-floor windows are almost always better done indoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patio Door Kits</h3>

          <p>
            Patio door kits are simply larger sheets, around 7 by 9 feet, so a sliding door can be covered without a seam. Indoor or outdoor, the door is out of use until you remove the film.
          </p>

