        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best window insulation kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the 3M Indoor Window Insulator Kit five-pack is the best window insulation kit: it fits five standard 3 by 5 foot windows, shrinks clear with a hair dryer, and uses Scotch mounting tape that owners commonly report holds all season. With pets or kids, the thicker Duck Max Strength kit resists punctures better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do window insulation kits really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on drafty and single-pane windows. The taped film stops air leaking around the sashes and traps a layer of still air against the glass, which cuts drafts, warms the glass and reduces condensation and frost. On modern sealed double-pane windows the improvement is much smaller, so the biggest gains come in older homes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is indoor or outdoor window insulation better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Indoor is better for most homes. You work from inside at room temperature, the film shrinks clear with a hair dryer, and it is protected from wind and snow. Outdoor kits make sense when indoor trim is delicate or freshly painted, or the indoor frame cannot hold tape, and only on windows you can reach safely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will window insulation tape peel paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, especially on fresh, soft or poorly bonded paint. Test a short strip on a hidden spot first, give new paint time to cure, and consider a low-tack painter's tape base layer on doubtful trim. In spring, warm the tape with a hair dryer and peel it slowly back on itself at a low angle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does window insulation film last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Shrink film is a one-season product. It stays tight through a winter if the tape was applied to a clean, dry frame, but most people remove it in spring to open the windows, and Frost King's listing notes that sunlight and normal stress can eventually make the tape fail. Plan on applying new film each autumn.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you see through window insulation film?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Once shrunk tight with a hair dryer, good indoor film is clear enough that most people stop noticing it, and Duck and 3M both describe their film as crystal clear or clear as glass. Stretch-film outdoor kits are usually a little less clear because they do not pull as wrinkle-free.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a window insulation kit on a patio door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a kit large enough to cover the whole frame in one sheet, such as the 3M Patio Door kit sized for doors up to 6 feet 8 inches by 9 feet. The catch is that the door cannot be opened until you remove the film, so only seal a door you will not use or need as an exit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
